\title{readfiscalnz: Reproducible New Zealand Treasury fiscal data}
\description{
\code{readfiscalnz} turns six exact New Zealand Treasury Excel publications into
validated, long-form R data. The supported material comprises two workbooks
containing long histories, one point-in-time monthly accounts release, and
three separate Economic and Fiscal Update forecast vintages. Fiscal regimes,
accounting basis, consolidation scope, units, and actual/forecast status
remain explicit in the returned data.
}
\details{
\subsection{Supported release families}{

The package covers four Treasury families and six publication artifacts:
\itemize{
\item \code{hfi-1972-2025} is one current published snapshot of the rolling
Historical Fiscal Indicators history across 1972--2025, not an archive of
HFI publication vintages;
\item \code{tax-outturn-2026-05} is one current published snapshot of the rolling
monthly revenue and receipts history through May 2026;
\item \code{monthly-financials-2026-02} is one point-in-time Financial Statements
release for the eight months ended 28 February 2026, with annual,
year-to-date, actual, forecast, and variance observation roles; and
\item \code{befu-2026}, \code{hyefu-2025}, and \code{prefu-2023} are three separate Economic
and Fiscal Update vintages with fiscal, economic, and key-indicator sheets
containing both actual or unaudited rows and forecast rows.
}

Each identifier maps to one original, byte-pinned Treasury \code{.xlsx} file.
With no \code{file} argument, the corresponding reader resolves the catalogued
Treasury URL after terms acknowledgement and may reuse the exact
hash-addressed raw cache before downloading. With \code{file}, it parses a
caller-supplied local \code{.xlsx} or canonical \code{.csv}. The six full workbooks are
not installed with the package; installed official data are limited to the
documented 32-row HFI Core Crown Expenses canonical CSV subset, not the
original XLSX. Other installed CSV examples are synthetic. Use
\code{\link[=list_nz_fiscal_releases]{list_nz_fiscal_releases()}} to inspect exact filenames, release status,
dates, source URLs, hashes, forecast horizons, and per-release terms.
}

\subsection{Typical workflow}{
\enumerate{
\item Discover a supported release with \code{\link[=list_nz_fiscal_releases]{list_nz_fiscal_releases()}}.
\item Read it with the corresponding \verb{read_nz_*()} function: \code{\link[=read_nz_hfi]{read_nz_hfi()}},
\code{\link[=read_nz_tax_outturn]{read_nz_tax_outturn()}}, \code{\link[=read_nz_monthly_financials]{read_nz_monthly_financials()}}, or
\code{\link[=read_nz_efu]{read_nz_efu()}}. A saved XLSX or canonical CSV can be supplied through
\code{file} for deterministic offline replay.
\item Identify structural series with \code{\link[=nz_fiscal_series_catalogue]{nz_fiscal_series_catalogue()}} and
replay one ordered series with \code{\link[=nz_fiscal_series_history]{nz_fiscal_series_history()}}.
\item Audit the source with \code{\link[=nz_fiscal_provenance]{nz_fiscal_provenance()}} and, when needed, verify
the exact preserved bytes with \code{\link[=nz_fiscal_raw_bytes]{nz_fiscal_raw_bytes()}}. Inspect licensing
metadata with \code{\link[=nz_fiscal_terms]{nz_fiscal_terms()}}.
}
}

\subsection{Output and provenance}{

Every reader returns a long-form object with class
\code{c("nz_fiscal_data", "data.frame")}. Each row is an observation with a
family-specific set of dimensions and a numeric \code{value}; date fields are
\code{Date} values (\code{date} for tax outturn and \code{period_end} for the other
families). The dimensions retain the source's series, section or table,
accounting and consolidation semantics, units, fiscal regime, and
actual/forecast or publication-status labels. The object carries an
\code{nz_fiscal_provenance} attribute containing the selected release, source
route, licensing metadata, revision policy, raw SHA-256 and size, and the
parser and schema versions. These values are available through
\code{\link[=nz_fiscal_provenance]{nz_fiscal_provenance()}} rather than inferred from parsed rows.
}

\subsection{Network, cache, and terms}{

Package load and the default test suite do not perform network requests.
Supplying \code{file} never performs network I/O. Otherwise, a reader resolves
the catalogued official artifact over HTTPS only, and refuses retrieval
until \code{accept_terms = TRUE} has been supplied after reviewing
\code{\link[=nz_fiscal_terms]{nz_fiscal_terms()}}. Accepted artifacts are signature- and SHA-256-checked
before parsing. Raw snapshots are written below the package-managed
\code{tools::R_user_dir("readfiscalnz", "cache")/v1} cache by default; parsed
results are kept in a bounded in-memory cache. Set \code{cache_dir} to a
temporary or project-managed directory for isolated workflows, and use
\code{\link[=prune_nz_fiscal_cache]{prune_nz_fiscal_cache()}} to apply raw-cache limits. Local replay may write
to that cache but never modifies the input file.
}

